2. THE FIXED INTERFACE (read where it is written; restated, never amended)
   CON-004 (REQUIREMENTS-TRACE.md, constraint): "Each I/O supervisor has its own regulator branch, reset supervisor, watchdog, crystal and SWD pads, and the three talk over two independent CAN-FD fabrics with separate transceivers and termination."
     accepted when: "Netlist shows three independent supply branches and two fabrics; A7 (either fabric cut, the quorum holds) passes."
     fabrics it names: 2; this round keeps both, adds none and removes none
   IOHA row 3: "One I/O supervisor dies or is unpowered" -> "the other two are a majority and ownership is unaffected" (detected by: its heartbeat stops on both fabrics; its status LED is dark)
   IOHA row 4: "Two supervisors die" -> "no majority; every voted bit reads its pull-down and the fabric rests on the home assignment, which is the board's behaviour before this work" (detected by: heartbeat)
   IOHA row 5: "A supervisor wedges with its outputs stuck" -> "outvoted 2 of 3; it cannot move a single bit" (detected by: the other two see its heartbeat stop or its votes disagree with quorum)
   IOHA row 6: "A voter output stuck at one value" -> "that bank cannot move, or is stuck on the failover host" (detected by: **each controller reads the four voted bits back**, so a vote that does not match quorum is visible)
   IOHA row 7: "One CAN fabric breaks or a transceiver fails dominant" -> "quorum continues on the other fabric" (detected by: bus-off and error counters on the broken fabric)
   IOHA row 8: "Both CAN fabrics break" -> "no controller can form a majority; firmware rule is that a controller without quorum does not act, so the voters hold the home assignment" (detected by: each controller sees both fabrics silent)
   IOHA test A7: "cut fabric A, then fabric B, one at a time" -> "quorum survives each; the broken fabric reports bus-off; with both broken nothing moves"
     as drawn (gen_sch_b.py): controller A sits alone on its segment behind each fabric's break links (R508/R509, R511/R512), so A7's
     cut separates A from B and C, who keep that fabric; with both links out only A is cut off (row R8c below, finding W139-F4)
   FW-B22 as drafted (apply_hw_fw_contract_t10.py, unapplied): "In every 100 ms window each supervisor sends on each fabric one state frame (its view of the assignment, its health, a sequence count) and at most five event frames, classic frames of at most 8 data bytes; a larger change spreads over windows; the 2-of-3 decision reads the three state frames of one window"
   the compatibility page's DAR row: "| 2.24.5 DAR mode transmission failure due to lost arbitration | A / A / A | only in DAR (no automatic retransmission) mode | firmware does not use DAR |"
   M-B as drafted by W137 (inputs/l4canmb-l9t5_canmb-5d14cc85.out, fnd/l4canmb 0d079eaf, unchecked): each TXD buffered (74LVC1G34 on
     its own rail) to its two peers' TIM3 captures through 2.2 kOhm; each TXD held recessive in reset by 10 kOhm; twelve votes, one per
     transceiver per peer, into an SN74LVC1G08 2-of-2 gate on the target's rail lifting SHDN through a diode; the share limiter removed;
     a dark controller reads RECESSIVE at its readers; one reader cannot frame a healthy controller; silence within 10.020 us of the
     second vote, released within 13.01 us; the self-test's cycle 2.4000 s, a latent fault DETECTED WITHIN 5.00 s
     its four residuals: a vote pull-down (10 kOhm) open; SD's 100 kOhm open; an isolation resistor short; a TXD pull-up (10 kOhm) open
   W138's regulator stage (inputs/l4reg-l4reg_compare-9fbda7a6.out, fnd/l4reg 86dbcdff, unchecked): TPS2553-1, IOS 0.4702 to 0.5704 A,
     latch-off after 5 to 10 ms (SLVS841F 7.5, PRINTED as that record quotes it; the held sheet is not read here); 'nothing in
     this draft drives EN'; row 8's held states 0.5491 A ((f2)) and 0.5639 A (rev V held, t10 10e) lie inside the band

3. THE PRINTED FIGURES THE TIMING RESTS ON
   tTXD_DTO, driver dominant time out: 1.2 ms minimum, 3.8 ms maximum (SLLSEQ7F 5.6)
   tMODE, mode change time: 10 us maximum (SLLSEQ7F 5.6)
   'The CAN protocol allows a maximum of eleven successive dominant bits (on TXD) for the worst case, where five successive dominant bits are followed immediately by an error frame' (SLLSEQ7F 5.6, note 1)
   Table 6-5: SHDN HIGH, driver Disabled (OFF), receiver Disabled (OFF), RXD High (Recessive)
   unpowered: 'Bus and logic pins are high impedance (no load to operating bus or application)' (SLLSEQ7F 1)
   Table 160 (HSLV OFF), speed 00, tr/tf at 50 pF and 2.7 to 3.6 V: 16.6 ns maximum (DS12110 Rev 10)
   tpd A or B to Y at 3.3 V +-0.3 V, 30 or 50 pF: 4.5 ns maximum to 85 C, 6 ns to 125 C (SCES217AA 5.8)
   'In bus monitoring mode (For more details refer to ISO11898-1, 10.12 bus monitoring), the FDCAN is able to receive valid data frames and valid remote frames, but cannot start a transmission. In this mode, it sends only recessive bits on the CAN bus' (RM0433 Rev 8 p.2469)
   'In DAR mode all transmissions are automatically canceled after they started on the CAN bus.' (RM0433 Rev 8 p.2470)
   FDCAN_TSCC TSS[1:0] = 10: 'External timestamp counter from TIM3 value used (tim3_cnt[0:15])', and 'The internal/external timestamp counter value is captured on start of frame (both Rx and Tx)' (RM0433 Rev 8 pp.2529 and 2530)
   after Bus_Off, once INIT is cleared, 'the device waits for 129 occurrences of bus Idle (129 x 11 consecutive recessive bits)' (RM0433 Rev 8 p.2534)
   FDCAN_IR bit 27 PEA, 'Protocol error in arbitration phase detected (PSR.LEC different from 0,7)' (RM0433 Rev 8 p.2535)
   ES0392 Rev 15 2.24.5: 'In DAR mode, the transmission may fail due to lost arbitration at the first two identifier bits.' Workaround: 'Upon failure, clear the corresponding Tx buffer transmission request bit TRPx of the FDCAN_TXBRP register and set the corresponding cancellation finished bit CFx of the FDCAN_TXBCF register, then restart the transmission.'
   RM0433 Rev 8 pp.2532 and 2533: error passive at 128, Error_Warning at 96, TEC 0 to 255 (PRINTED); the increments (+8 a failed
     transmission, -1 a success) are ISO 11898-1's, which is not held: ASSUMPTION

4. THE ATTRIBUTION RULE AND THE CONTAINMENT CHAIN (DRAFTED for FW-B22 restated; the hardware figures PRINTED)
   each controller reads both other controllers' TXDs on both fabrics (its own it knows), so it sees every transmitter's intent;
   a target's TXD activity is OUT OF CONTRACT (a violation) when, on one fabric, it shows:
     V1 a dominant run longer than 11 bit-times plus 0.25 us (TI: the protocol allows at most eleven successive dominant bits on TXD)
     V2 edges closer than half a bit-time (faster than any CAN bit stream)
     V3 a malformed stream where the target owns the frame (stuffing, a fixed field, its CRC, decoded from its TXD)
     V4 a dominant bit where the frame's owner sends recessive, other than the ACK slot, before any other deviation in that frame
        (the FIRST deviation: the flags of the controllers that answer it begin after it and are legal)
     V5 a start of frame outside its own slot, or more frames in a window than its slot allows (1 state and 5 event frames)
   ONE TIME BASE: the reader's TIM3 time-stamps the peers' TXD edges (W137's capture channels) and, with FDCAN_TSCC.TSS = 10, its own
     FDCANs' start of every frame sent or received (RM0433 p.2529), so the TXDs and its own frames are compared on one counter
   a STRIKE needs the violation AND the reader's own evidence on that fabric: its FDCAN's protocol error (FDCAN_IR.PEA, PSR.LEC) or its
     own scheduled frame lost, inside the frame the violation hit, explained by the target's deviation; NEVER a TXD reading alone: a
     dark controller (its buffer reads recessive), a stuck buffer or a stuck reading input moves no bus bit and yields no strike;
     an error nobody's TXD explains (a physical fabric fault) yields no strike: FW-B21's stop handles it
   a reader votes on its own strike (k = 1), never on a message received over a fabric (a jammer may deny both); the 2-of-2 gate needs
     both peers' own strikes, so no single faulty controller silences a healthy one
   the chain at 500 kbit/s: evidence 300.0 us (the frame's end, 150 bit-times, MODEL) + evaluation 1.000 ms (DRAFTED) + the vote's edge 16.6 ns
     (PRINTED) + the gate 6.0 ns (PRINTED, to 125 C) + tMODE 10 us (PRINTED) = 1.310 ms from a deviation to the target's driver off;
     a held TXD: 1.034 ms (V1 at 12 bit-times); at 1 Mbit/s 1.160 ms and 1.022 ms; the TCAN334's own DTO frees a held bus after 1.2 to 3.8 ms
   the vote is held 1.000 s, doubled for each strike within 10.000 s of a release, at most 64.000 s; a healthy controller's error counter: each
     lost own frame +8 (ASSUMPTION), so the containment acts long before FW-B21's stop or error passive (128, PRINTED)

5. FW-B22 RESTATED ON THE TWO FABRICS (DRAFTED, apply_hw_fw_contract_canq.py; CON-004 restated, not amended)
   the window 100.000 ms on each fabric: guard 0 to 10 ms; state slots A 10.000 ms, B 20.000 ms, C 30.000 ms, each 10.000 ms, a controller's frames only in its own
     slot (1 state frame and at most 5 event frames, as drafted), so healthy controllers never arbitrate against each other
   the self-test's hold 52.000 ms to 88.000 ms in its own segment with one test frame per controller at 60.000 ms, 70.000 ms and 80.000 ms: no state frame is ever
     silenced by the test (W137's draft holds 10 to 90 ms with the state frames at mid-window); both fabrics tested in parallel, fabric B's
     target the next controller; a cycle is 3 targets x 4 phases = 12 windows; the restated V phase runs the attribution path end to
     end (the target's deliberate malformed test frame, struck by both readers, their votes acting to the hold's end)
   the decision: a peer's state frame for window n taken from EITHER fabric, counted only in its sender's slot with its sender's
     identifier (a frame carrying another controller's identifier is discarded); two copies that differ discard that peer's state for n;
     a peer is lost after 3 consecutive windows with no valid state frame on either fabric; 2 of 3 as drafted
   DAR = 1 kept (L9T5-D2) with ES0392 2.24.5's printed workaround inside the controller's own slot: with slots healthy controllers
     never lose arbitration to each other, so 2.24.5 is met only against a faulty one; the compatibility page's 'firmware does not
     use DAR' is to be restated (W137-F1; finding W139-F1); row F1 below shows why DAR = 0 does not serve the self-test
   FW-B21's stop RESTATED at the loss count: 'no valid frame for more than 3 windows' in place of the drafted 'for 100 ms', which is one
     window of this schedule: one lost state frame then stopped a fabric for a second and the GPIO jammer of M1c took the quorum (row
     F2); the stop's delay moves no thermal figure, because "each window carries at most 2 ms of own dominant drive by the schedule, so a fault's current is averaged over the window whatever the detection time" (t10 10h (3))
   a returning controller (a reset, a latch released, power back) listens in bus monitoring mode (MON) for 2 windows, takes the epoch
     and the window's timing from the state frames, sets its votes and voted outputs to the read-back, and only then sends: it moves nothing

